WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.540
Film Courage: Continuing on with fighting
for the work.

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:07.310
Let’s suppose you’re working with someone
and they come to you and you either have someone

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:13.420
who is self-deprecating and “mouse-like”
or someone who is more of an over-confident

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:14.650
braggart.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:17.449
Either one is probably detrimental to getting
their work out.

00:00:17.449 --> 00:00:18.449
Lee Jessup: Yes!

00:00:18.449 --> 00:00:23.740
Film Courage: So what are your rules for either
breaking down the braggart or maybe you’re

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:24.740
not doing this?

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:26.270
Or boosting up the mouse?

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:34.640
Lee Jessup: With the mouse…I just talked
to one such mouse this morning who takes very

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:43.010
failure, every “no” as a testament to
her own life long, predestined failure.

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:47.420
And so I have very hard brutal conversations
about the fact that there will be a lot of

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:50.300
rejections, there will be a lot of no’s
at every level.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:52.510
At every level of this industry, nothing is
ever perfect.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:55.280
Nothing is just ever handed to you.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.239
And so it’s important to me to have a lot
of candid conversations about learning not

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:06.270
to take the no’s with quite as much weight,
because if you do, it’s just never ending,

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:07.270
right?

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:09.210
It’s just a recipe for disaster.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:12.980
So we talk a lot about conviction in the work,
believing in your work, believing in your

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:18.299
potential, not needing to rely on other people
for your own self-worth.

00:01:18.299 --> 00:01:20.790
Because really, that’s what it boils down
to.

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:26.280
And so we talk a lot about that and fattening
up people’s lives.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:32.270
So making sure that my writers that are mousey
have their sources, their dependable sources

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:33.270
of fun.

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:35.450
That it’s not about the writing all the
time.

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:38.329
Because the other side of it is, if it’s
just the writing, if it’s all about the

00:01:38.329 --> 00:01:42.240
writing, all the time and nothing else, eventually
they will have nothing else to invest in the

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:43.240
writing itself.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:44.840
They will have nothing to bring to the writing.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:48.580
So it’s really kind of filling our their
lives and making sure they they are fulfilled

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:52.990
on many different levels and it’s not just
the writing that defines them.

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:57.820
Because if it is only the writing that defines
you, A) you will become a very boring writer

00:01:57.820 --> 00:01:58.820
and B)

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:02.689
it will never be fulfilling enough ever…and
I’ve seen that a million times.

00:02:02.689 --> 00:02:06.659
Hemingway committed…there is something to
learn there.

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:11.320
And so the ones who are…tend to be a little
bit more “braggy.”

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:17.350
Tend to be a little bit more egotistical,
a bit more full of themselves, we will talk

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:24.010
a lot about how their behavior is coming off,
and I will do a lot of meeting prep with them

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:30.990
and “the way you are talking about that
(to me) comes off a certain way” and you

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:32.580
may want to pay attention to these things.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:33.580
That’s why I am here.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:37.050
I am here to reflect that and be a mirror
to those things.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:43.250
And so we will certainly talk about how those
things come off and that we can’t afford

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:44.250
that.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:47.660
Nobody wants to work with someone who is completely
full of themselves.

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:53.670
I had recently a conversation with a manager
about a client of mine who he represents.

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:59.500
And he says “I think this client couldn’t
care less about my opinion.”

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:02.200
And I said “Well, actually I think it is
quite the contrary.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:06.040
I think that he is desperate to impress you.

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:11.220
And I think because of that he may be coming
off a little bit more bigger and boastful

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:17.870
but it’s out of desperation to get you on
his side, to get you to rally behind him.

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:22.140
It’s not for lack of respect, it’s because
of respect that he’s behaving that way.”

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:27.620
So many times writers don’t have me as a
go-between to communicate to their agents

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:32.190
or their managers or the executive that they
are working with so a lot of it is me talking

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:34.790
to them as “Listen, this is how I am reading
you right now.

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:37.761
I know it doesn’t sound fun and I’m sorry
to say this and I love you and I know what

00:03:37.761 --> 00:03:40.320
a great person you are, but this is how I
am reading this.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:41.650
Is this how you want me to read this?

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:44.230
If you are fine with it, keep going.

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:51.950
But I am reading this as someone who thinks
they are smarter than anyone else in the room.”

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:53.050
Nobody wants to work with that.

00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:55.090
Film Courage: Can I hear some examples?

00:03:55.090 --> 00:04:00.760
Let’s suppose conversations with the self-deprecating
writer versus conversations with the braggart,

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:06.040
because maybe this person doesn’t recognize
who they are?

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:10.870
The braggart….the most obvious and overt
ones are the braggarts that will come in and

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:13.510
say (and these are usually not professional
writers.

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:17.530
Professional writers don’t do this and have
learned better and have broken because they

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:18.530
don’t.

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:19.650
But it has happened).

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:22.530
“This is the greatest script you are ever
going to read!”

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:27.380
“I am the Michael Jordan of screenwriting.”

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:28.380
Whoah!

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:33.490
Hold on…these are things that have been
said to me, for the record.

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:36.570
“This is the script you’ve been waiting
for.”

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:41.190
“Take this script on vacation.

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:43.650
This is my vacation gift to you.”

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:50.980
“I want you sit at sunset and read it on
the veranda because this will make your vacation.”

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:52.790
So those things have been said to me.

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:59.110
You know, you want to steer clear of “This
is the greatest, this is the best script ever!”

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:02.250
You can certainly say “This is the greatest
thing I’ve ever written, I’m really proud

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:05.750
of it, I’ve worked really hard at it.

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:09.400
You know, I have eight scripts behind me and
this is my best one.”

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:13.190
That’s not braggery, that is genuine.

00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:14.690
But somebody coming in and saying “Oh, my
Gosh.

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:17.460
This is so much better than anything else
out there.”

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:24.250
I had one writer, a working writer, no longer
a working writer, who used to be very, very

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:31.550
successful, had fallen from grace (a bit,
not a great deal) but who used to every year

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:36.780
write a list of all the screenplays that were
nominated for academy awards and then write

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:42.640
arguments for why his script was better and
send it out in Christmas cards.

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:46.110
Not a good idea!

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:47.800
Just not a good idea.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:50.900
If you are saying to me that your script,
that is unproduced, that nobody wants to pick

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:57.860
up, that has been read by everybody, but nobody
loved it, is better than ARGO, you’re…hhmmm.

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:00.620
It’s a problem.

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:05.780
So that’s the braggery side and of course
the examples I’ve brought in are a little

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:11.050
bit on the extreme side of it, but I think
you get the tone of it.

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:19.960
The more self-defeating writers, it’s usually
conversation about “I got this rejection.

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:23.080
I know that I will never be the person I want
to be.”

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:30.160
There’s usually a direct connection about
what rejection means about the person, not

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:32.750
about the script or the person reading the
script.

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:35.550
“I’m not going to make it as a writer.

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:36.870
I will never be happy.

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:39.060
I will never be fulfilled.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:41.700
I’m just a loser.

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:43.389
I didn’t make it in this contest.”

00:06:43.389 --> 00:06:48.700
I can’t tell you the rhyme or reason of
screenwriting contest.

00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:51.530
I’ve been in this industry forever and I
still don’t understand them.

00:06:51.530 --> 00:06:55.460
And I have writers say “I didn’t make
the quarter finals in the Nichol, I’m a

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:58.980
loser, I know that I’m a loser now, I’m
a bad writer.”

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:03.010
Hold on, there’s going to be lots more rejections,
there’s going to be lots more opportunities

00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:04.030
to be a loser.

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:06.620
Don’t jump at the chance so quickly.

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:08.389
But for a lot of writers it’s very real.

00:07:08.389 --> 00:07:14.370
And don’t get me wrong, every writer has
a moment or 15 of being self-defeatist.

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:17.951
You know, I have one writer who is a working
writer who is always saying if you’re not

00:07:17.951 --> 00:07:21.600
thinking about quitting at least twice a week
you are doing something wrong.

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:22.600
And I believe him.

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:23.980
I think it’s very, very true.

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:29.760
I think every writer thinks they are failing
at a moment, every writer thinks that they

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:35.180
are not good enough, every writer thinks that
they are never going to recreate the past

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:37.440
success that they’ve had.

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:44.930
I think that’s perfectly part of the deal,
but seeing everything as a failure, as an

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:47.230
indication that you are just a loser.

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:49.180
Or that you are just a bad writer.

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:51.660
Or you are just a bad person.

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:53.949
That is what I see in the more self-defeated
writers.

00:07:53.949 --> 00:07:57.010
And the problem is that nobody wants to work
with that.

00:07:57.010 --> 00:07:58.660
Nobody wants to be in a room with that.

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:03.400
Nobody wants to pick up your script as a validation
to who you are as a human being.

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:06.850
They want to pick it up your script because
it’s a great script and they want to bring

00:08:06.850 --> 00:08:09.510
you in the room because they think you have
something to contribute.

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:14.740
Not because they think that you need the room
and that’s a big distinction.

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:19.419
Film Courage: So are you giving these people
pep talks or that’s not really what your

00:08:19.419 --> 00:08:20.419
job is about?

00:08:20.419 --> 00:08:24.250
Lee Jessup: Oh…I give people pep talks…I’m
Israeli, I’m married to a New Yorker, so

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:27.880
there’s a lot of tough love in the picture.

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:32.380
You know, admittedly I get a lot of tears
from a lot of people and I want to be the

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:38.110
safe space to that and the place for writers
to voice frustration and I certainly try to

00:08:38.110 --> 00:08:39.500
support them through it.

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:44.840
There are writers where if I see that we are
getting to a place where every time we meet

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:51.590
it is just horrible and the writer is in pieces,
I will ask “Why in the world are you doing

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:52.590
this?”

00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:55.400
It’s more important to be happy than be
a writer, what are you doing?

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:57.510
I will have those conversations.

00:08:57.510 --> 00:09:01.300
There are pep talks, there is tough love and
there is a shoulder to give.

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:04.560
I recognize that this is a really, really
hard profession.

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:09.890
You know, I was talking to a writer this morning
who was really frustrated with a set of notes

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:15.210
that she was given on a pilot that she just
sold but the draft that she turned in, she

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:16.960
wasn’t delighted with.

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:20.590
And it took a toll on her because she really
wants to do the best at every thing.

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:22.380
And it brought her to the question “Why
am I doing this?”

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:27.240
And this is not her first time to the rodeo
and “Should I really keep going because

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:29.180
this happens every time?”

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:33.880
And I said to her “You know, the day you
sold this pilot, you called me on the drive

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:38.960
home after the pilot sold in the room and
you told me that this was one of the happiest

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:40.560
days in your life.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:43.850
Now you are having a bad day, this is not
one of the worst days of your life and because

00:09:43.850 --> 00:09:46.170
of that you are going to keep going.”

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:48.610
And that’s the truth of the matter, we are
all going to have bad days here and I’m

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:52.850
here to support my writers though them but
it’s about understanding when there are

00:09:52.850 --> 00:09:54.740
some bad days and when it’s all bad days.

